\title{Deepening the Algebraic Core of Four-Dimensional Geometry}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A single elementary identity, $(a+b)^2 = 4ab + (a-b)^2$, expresses the balance
between a sum and a difference of squares. We show that this identity, together
with its higher-order relatives, forms a compact algebraic core from which a
surprising amount of four-dimensional geometry can be reconstructed. We develop
the core in four directions. First, we record the composition identities of the
normed division algebras: the Brahmagupta--Fibonacci two-square identity
(multiplicativity of the complex modulus), Euler's four-square identity
(multiplicativity of the quaternion norm), and the three-variable Lagrange
identity that yields the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality. Second, we give a complete
characterization of the fibres of the Hopf map: two points of the unit
three-sphere have the same image if and only if they differ by a single unit
complex scalar, so that the fibres are \emph{exactly} the great circles. The
nontrivial direction is proved by an explicit division-free witness, the
Hermitian inner product $\lambda = \bar z z' + \bar w w'$. Third, we establish
that multiplication by $i$ on $\C^n$ is a norm-preserving, fixed-point-free
complex structure in every even dimension. Fourth, we prove the two- and
three-radius arithmetic--geometric mean bounds governing balanced Clifford tori,
and the rigidity statement that conjugation by any nonzero quaternion is an
isometry, the algebraic source of the rotation groups $SO(3)$ and $SO(4)$. The
unifying theme is that the geometry surrounding the Hopf fibration is organized by
the composition algebras, and that the phase ambiguity of a unit vector --
recovered locally from two points by an inner product -- is the true bridge
between algebra and the projective geometry of the fibration.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Four-dimensional geometry has a reputation for being unvisualizable, yet many of
its central objects are governed by algebra of striking simplicity. This paper
takes as its organizing principle a single identity,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:core}
(a+b)^2 = 4ab + (a-b)^2,
\end{equation}
which trivially rearranges the square of a sum. Our thesis is that
\eqref{eq:core} and a small family of related sum-of-squares identities
constitute an algebraic core from which the Hopf fibration, the complex structure
of even-dimensional space, the geometry of Clifford tori, and quaternionic
rotations all follow with little additional machinery.

The connective tissue is the theory of \emph{composition algebras} (equivalently,
normed division algebras): the reals $\R$, the complex numbers $\C$, the
quaternions $\HH$, and the octonions $\mathbb{O}$, of dimensions $1, 2, 4, 8$. In
each, a norm $\N$ satisfies $\N(xy) = \N(x)\N(y)$, and writing this
multiplicativity in coordinates produces exactly the two-, four-, and eight-square
identities. Hurwitz's theorem asserts that the list is complete: no bilinear
composition identity exists in any other dimension. The privileged status of
dimension four in geometry is a direct reflection of the existence of $\HH$.

\section{Preliminaries and notation}

We work over $\R$, $\C$, and the real quaternions $\HH$. For $z \in \C$ we write
$\bar z$ for the complex conjugate and $\N(z) = |z|^2 = z\bar z$ for the squared
modulus, so that $\N(z_1 z_2) = \N(z_1)\N(z_2)$ and $\N(\bar z) = \N(z)$. The unit
three-sphere is realized as
\[
S^3 = \{(z,w) \in \C^2 : \N(z) + \N(w) = 1\},
\]
and the unit two-sphere $S^2 \subset \C \times \R$ as the set of $(\zeta, t)$ with
$\N(\zeta) + t^2 = 1$. For quaternions $q \in \HH$ we write $\N(q)$ for the
(multiplicative) quaternion norm. Vectors in $\C^n$ are functions
$v : \{1,\dots,n\} \to \C$ with squared Euclidean norm $\sum_i \N(v_i)$.

\section{Composition identities and Cauchy--Schwarz}

\begin{proposition}[Sum-of-squares core]\label{prop:core}
For all real $a, b$, $(a+b)^2 = 4ab + (a-b)^2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Expand: $(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2$ and $4ab + (a-b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Brahmagupta--Fibonacci identity]
For all real $a,b,c,d$,
\[
(a^2+b^2)(c^2+d^2) = (ac-bd)^2 + (ad+bc)^2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Interpret $a^2+b^2 = \N(a+bi)$ and $c^2+d^2 = \N(c+di)$. Then $(a+bi)(c+di) =
(ac-bd) + (ad+bc)i$, and the identity is $\N(z_1 z_2) = \N(z_1)\N(z_2)$ in
coordinates.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Euler's four-square identity]\label{thm:four}
For all real $a_1,\dots,a_4,b_1,\dots,b_4$,
\begin{align*}
&(a_1^2+a_2^2+a_3^2+a_4^2)(b_1^2+b_2^2+b_3^2+b_4^2) \\
&= (a_1b_1 - a_2b_2 - a_3b_3 - a_4b_4)^2 + (a_1b_2 + a_2b_1 + a_3b_4 - a_4b_3)^2 \\
&\quad + (a_1b_3 - a_2b_4 + a_3b_1 + a_4b_2)^2 + (a_1b_4 + a_2b_3 - a_3b_2 + a_4b_1)^2.
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The four bilinear forms are the components of the quaternion product
$(a_1 + a_2 i + a_3 j + a_4 k)(b_1 + b_2 i + b_3 j + b_4 k)$, and the identity is
$\N(pq) = \N(p)\N(q)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Lagrange's identity]\label{thm:lagrange}
For all real $a_1,a_2,a_3,b_1,b_2,b_3$,
\begin{align*}
&(a_1^2+a_2^2+a_3^2)(b_1^2+b_2^2+b_3^2) - (a_1b_1 + a_2b_2 + a_3b_3)^2 \\
&= (a_1b_2 - a_2b_1)^2 + (a_1b_3 - a_3b_1)^2 + (a_2b_3 - a_3b_2)^2.
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct expansion; the left side is the Gram determinant of $\mathbf{a},
\mathbf{b} \in \R^3$ and the right side is $\|\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}\|^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cauchy--Schwarz in $\R^3$]
For all real vectors $\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b} \in \R^3$,
\[
(a_1b_1 + a_2b_2 + a_3b_3)^2 \le (a_1^2+a_2^2+a_3^2)(b_1^2+b_2^2+b_3^2).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The right-hand side of Theorem~\ref{thm:lagrange} is a sum of squares, hence
nonnegative; rearranging yields the inequality, with equality iff $\mathbf{a}$
and $\mathbf{b}$ are parallel.
\end{proof}

\section{The Hopf fibration: fibres are exactly the great circles}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{Hopf map} $h : \C^2 \to \C \times \R$ is
$h(z,w) = \big(2 z \bar w,\; \N(z) - \N(w)\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Image on the sphere]\label{prop:image}
For all $z,w \in \C$,
$\N(2 z \bar w) + (\N(z) - \N(w))^2 = (\N(z) + \N(w))^2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Since $\N(2 z \bar w) = 4\N(z)\N(w)$, the claim is \eqref{eq:core} with
$a = \N(z)$, $b = \N(w)$.
\end{proof}

In particular, $h$ restricts to a map $S^3 \to S^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Circle invariance]\label{thm:inv}
For all $z,w,\lambda \in \C$ with $\N(\lambda) = 1$, $h(\lambda z, \lambda w) =
h(z,w)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$2(\lambda z)\overline{(\lambda w)} = 2\lambda\bar\lambda\, z\bar w = 2 z\bar w$
and $\N(\lambda z) - \N(\lambda w) = \N(\lambda)(\N(z) - \N(w)) = \N(z) - \N(w)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre converse]\label{thm:converse}
Let $(z,w),(z',w') \in S^3$ with $h(z,w) = h(z',w')$. Then there exists
$\lambda \in \C$ with $\N(\lambda) = 1$, $z' = \lambda z$, and $w' = \lambda w$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equality of Hopf images gives $2 z\bar w = 2 z'\bar{w'}$ and $\N(z) - \N(w) =
\N(z') - \N(w')$. With $\N(z) + \N(w) = 1 = \N(z') + \N(w')$, the second equation
forces $\N(z) = \N(z')$ and $\N(w) = \N(w')$. Take the division-free witness
\[
\lambda := \bar z\, z' + \bar w\, w'.
\]
Two bilinear computations, using $z\bar w = z'\bar{w'}$ and its conjugate together
with the norm relations, give $\lambda z = z'$ and $\lambda w = w'$. Finally
$\N(z') = \N(\lambda)\N(z)$ and $\N(w') = \N(\lambda)\N(w)$; summing,
$1 = \N(\lambda)(\N(z) + \N(w)) = \N(\lambda)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fibres are great circles]\label{thm:iff}
For $(z,w),(z',w') \in S^3$,
\[
h(z,w) = h(z',w') \iff \exists\,\lambda \in \C,\ \N(\lambda) = 1,\
z' = \lambda z,\ w' = \lambda w.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Forward: Theorem~\ref{thm:converse}. Reverse: Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}.
\end{proof}

Thus $h$ realizes the projective quotient $\C^2 \setminus \{0\} \to \mathbb{CP}^1
\cong S^2$ restricted to the unit sphere, and the fibres are exactly the great
circles $\{(\lambda z, \lambda w) : \N(\lambda) = 1\}$.

\begin{remark}[Sharpness]
The unit-sphere hypotheses are essential: without them, the diagonal scaling
$(z,w) \mapsto (tz,tw)$ multiplies $h$ by $t^2$, so points of different norm can
never share an image and the great-circle description fails.
\end{remark}

\section{The complex structure $J$ in every even dimension}

\begin{definition}
For $v \in \C^n$, define $Jv \in \C^n$ by $(Jv)_i = i\, v_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Complex structure]
$J^2 = -\mathrm{Id}$; that is, $(J(Jv))_i = -v_i$ for all $i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(J(Jv))_i = i(i v_i) = i^2 v_i = -v_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Isometry]
For all $v \in \C^n$, $\sum_i \N((Jv)_i) = \sum_i \N(v_i)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\N(i v_i) = \N(i)\N(v_i) = \N(v_i)$ termwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-point freeness]
If $\sum_i \N(v_i) = 1$, then $Jv \neq v$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $Jv = v$ then $(i-1)v_i = 0$ for all $i$; since $i - 1 \neq 0$, each $v_i = 0$,
contradicting $\sum_i \N(v_i) = 1$.
\end{proof}

These hold uniformly in $n$: $J$ is the general form of ``rotation through the
fourth dimension,'' the first quarter-turn that acts on a sphere with no invariant
axis.

\section{Balanced Clifford tori and quaternionic rigidity}

\begin{proposition}[Clifford balance]\label{prop:balance}
If $a + b = 1$, then $4ab = 1 - (a-b)^2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$1 = (a+b)^2 = 4ab + (a-b)^2$ by \eqref{eq:core}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Balanced extremum]
If $a + b = 1$, then $4ab = 1$ if and only if $a = b = \tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:balance}, $4ab = 1 \iff (a-b)^2 = 0 \iff a = b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Three-radius AM--GM bound]
If $a,b,c \ge 0$ and $a + b + c = 1$, then $abc \le \tfrac{1}{27}$, with equality
iff $a = b = c = \tfrac13$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the arithmetic--geometric mean inequality for three nonnegative reals
summing to $1$: it follows from the nonnegativity of $(a-b)^2,(b-c)^2,(a-c)^2$ and
of the pairwise products, expanding $(a+b+c)^3$ and comparing with $27abc$.
Equality forces all pairwise differences to vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conjugation is an isometry]\label{thm:conj}
For all $q,x \in \HH$ with $q \neq 0$, $\N(q x q^{-1}) = \N(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By multiplicativity, $\N(q x q^{-1}) = \N(q)\N(x)\N(q^{-1}) = \N(q)\N(x)/\N(q) =
\N(x)$, valid since $\N(q) \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

On unit quaternions, $x \mapsto q x q^{-1}$ fixes the real axis and rotates the
imaginary part, realizing the double cover $\HH^\times \to SO(3)$; the two-sided
action realizes $SO(4)$. The coordinate form of the multiplicativity used here is
Euler's identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:four}).

\section{Discussion}

The through-line is that the ``fourth dimension'' is organized by the composition
algebras $\R,\C,\HH,\mathbb{O}$. Each square-sum identity is the coordinate avatar
of norm-multiplicativity in one of these algebras, and each geometric theorem is a
shadow of that algebra: the two- and four-square identities are $\C$ and $\HH$ made
explicit; the Hopf fibration is the projective quotient $\C^2 \to \mathbb{CP}^1
\cong S^2$, with fibres recovered exactly from two points by the Hermitian inner
product; the complex structure $J$ is multiplication by the unit imaginary; and
quaternionic conjugation is the norm-preserving inner automorphism generating
$SO(3)$ and $SO(4)$. A fibre has two faces: invariance under a group action (easy)
and rigidity, the reconstruction of the group element from two orbit points (the
substantive step), resolved by the division-free witness $\lambda = \bar z z' +
\bar w w'$.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Octonionic Hopf map.} For $S^{15} \to S^8$, the $S^7$ fibres should be
recoverable from a single inner-product witness evaluated in a fixed associative
subalgebra of the octonions; non-associativity obstructs only the global group
structure, not local fibre reconstruction.

\textbf{Isoperimetric flat tori.} Among flat $m$-tori in $S^{2m-1}$ with squared
radii summing to one, the balanced torus $r_i^2 = 1/m$ should uniquely maximize
volume, growing like $m^{-m/2}$; the volume is monotone in $\prod r_i^2$, so
$m$-variable AM--GM controls the extremal problem.

\textbf{Fixed-point-free isometries.} Every fixed-point-free linear isometry of
$S^{2n-1}$ of order dividing $4$ should be conjugate to multiplication by $i$ on
$\C^n$: fixed-point freeness is the absence of eigenvalue $1$, which for order $4$
pairs the coordinates as a complex structure.

\section{Conclusion}

From the elementary identity $(a+b)^2 = 4ab + (a-b)^2$ we assembled a compact
algebraic core for four-dimensional geometry and pushed it to a complete
characterization of the Hopf fibres, a uniform treatment of the even-dimensional
complex structure, and sharp balance and rigidity results for Clifford tori and
quaternionic rotations. Norm-multiplicativity in the composition algebras is both
the algebraic content of the sum-of-squares identities and the geometric content
of the fibration, and Hurwitz's theorem explains why four is a distinguished
dimension.

